    "q-238051": {
      "dom_id": "step-q238051",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ p+q=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $q$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $q,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the coefficient of $q$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the coefficient of $q$ will turn into $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ (\\,p+q=4\\,)\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ -2p-2q=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3p+\\cancel{2q} & =11 \\\\ -2p-\\cancel{2q} & =-8 \\\\ (3p-2p) & =(11-8) \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $q,$ multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-2,$ and add the equations*.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
